      "writtenOn": "2020-02-19T21:28:31Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "now we have two bits of state for each uart pin, one globally accessible and one function-static.\n\nWould it be cleaner to consolidate into a single state variable with 2 (or even 8) bits per UART?\n\nThen we can track exactly what the bus is doing: Z, GP, TIM, UART, I2C, SPI, voltage, whatever...",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 50,
        "startChar": 0,
        "endLine": 50,
        "endChar": 77
      },
      "revId": "c12e2ef25086b39c19170a0310beb05ebd071671",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba",
      "unresolved": true
    },
    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "c29e00ae_c35f922d",
        "filename": "board/c2d2/board.c",
        "patchSetId": 2
      },
      "lineNbr": 50,
      "author": {
        "id": 1259838
      },
      "writtenOn": "2020-02-20T18:40:59Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "I think it actually makes it cleaner for the code to have the global \"lock\" on the pins and the locally scope functionality. Each functionality shouldn\u0027t care what anyone else is doing, only if it can get the global lock.\n\nI did try out a few different forms here to play around, but I couldn\u0027t find anything that improved readability in my opinion. Also there are two UART states: uart unlock (normal) and uart locked (held low) which starts making things looks a little weird.",
      "parentUuid": "ff2cc388_b543f23c",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 50,
        "startChar": 0,
        "endLine": 50,
        "endChar": 77
      },
      "revId": "c12e2ef25086b39c19170a0310beb05ebd071671",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba",
      "unresolved": false
    },
    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "83e1bdae_9f76bad7",
        "filename": "board/c2d2/board.c",
        "patchSetId": 2
      },
      "lineNbr": 141,
      "author": {
        "id": 1002022
      },
      "writtenOn": "2020-02-19T21:28:31Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "I like the ap name because it\u0027s 2 characters and matches the fact that it overrides the AP uart.  But I also don\u0027t like it because technically this I2C would never be connected to the AP; rather it would be used for the INAs or some other auxiliary function.\n\nIs naming the I2C \"ap\" consistent with previous servos, or is there a more appropriate name?",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 141,
        "startChar": 11,
        "endLine": 141,
        "endChar": 13
      },
      "revId": "c12e2ef25086b39c19170a0310beb05ebd071671",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba",
      "unresolved": true
    },
    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "e8cd1331_d5dfa658",
        "filename": "board/c2d2/board.c",
        "patchSetId": 2
      },
      "lineNbr": 141,
      "author": {
        "id": 1259838
      },
      "writtenOn": "2020-02-20T18:40:59Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "There is only a single i2c bus on other designs, and it is called master.\n\nI like ap because it is on the AP pins, but I agree that is it really the free form i2c bus. Naming AP does give the user indication that this in mutually exclusive with AP uart (or freeform UART that would traditionally be connected to ap).\n\nI am fine changing it if you have another suggestion though.",
      "parentUuid": "83e1bdae_9f76bad7",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 141,
        "startChar": 11,
        "endLine": 141,
        "endChar": 13
      },
